  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nZonal flow continues this morning turning northwesterly aloft this \nevening as a negative tilted ridge amplifies a bit over the western \nCONUS with an ongoing large troughing pattern in Canada. Quiet \nconditions expected through this afternoon as high pressure centers \nitself over the region. The high pressure system will exit towards \nthe southeast tonight into Monday morning as a surface trough, \nextending from a deepening low tracking across Canada, will track \neast/southeast across the region Monday followed by a cold front \nplunging southward out of Canada Tuesday.\n\nCAMS keep the storm potential spotty as a cluster of storms are \nforecast to move into north central and another area into \ncentral/south central SD, tracking southeast this evening through \ntonight. There is timing and coverage differences between the \nindividual CAMs with NBM doing a good job broadbrushing pops between \n35-45% mainly along and west of the Mo River. CAPE generally will be \nat and below 1000j/kg but shear will be around 30kts. A lack of a \nLLJ will keep the threat low for any organized severe storms or long \nlasting storms. With more of an isolated threat, the SPC has a \nMarginal Risk, level 1/5, for severe storms over portions of central \nSD this evening into tonight.\n\nAs the trough tracks eastward across the Dakotas Monday, CAMs \nindicate additional spotty chances of showers and thunderstorms \ncontinuing here and there, mainly east of the Mo River during the \nday (where the trough will be) and along and east of the James River \nin the afternoon and evening as the axis of the trough moves over \nthis area. Better development (and more of a threat for organized \nsevere storms) seems to occur to our east/southeast of the CWA, per \nHREF. Winds at 500mb will increase between 40-50kts Monday evening \nwith CAPE values increasing to around 1500j/kg over south\ncentral to northeastern SD/MN, bulk shear of around 30-40kts, \nwith dps in the 60s. However, there is not much in a way of a \nLLJ as speeds are forecast below 20kts nor steep lapse rates. \nSo this keeps down the threat for long lived severe storms \nthrough the overnight hours. Due to this, the SPC has \nhighlighted a Marginal Risk again (level 1/5) due to this \nisolated threat mainly along and east of a line from Philip to \nSisseton. Hail 1\" in diameter and 60 mph wind gusts are possible\nalong with brief heavy downpours.\n\nThe cold front from this system will plunge southward Monday evening \ninto Tuesday bringing additional shower and thunderstorms chances. \nBehind the system, shortwave energy continues and if it can line up \nwith the surface boundaries over the region, additional threat for \nrain and storms continues through the end of the week.",
